<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 605–<a href="/years/632">632 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fatima was the daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and wife of Ali. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> took its name from her, as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> claimed descent from her and Ali, a claim that was central to their religious legitimacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fatima.jpg" alt="Fatima" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: کانون هنر شیعی · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FatimaalZahra_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Three conditions enabled the founding: a highly organised subversive network, a militarily capable Berber host people, and a theological claim tracing the imamate directly to the Prophet through his daughter <b>Fatima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Into this country came Idris ibn Abdallah, a great-grandson of Hasan ibn Ali and so a descendant of Ali ibn Abi Talib and <b>Fatima</b>, the daughter of the prophet Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tajlu Khanum, Ismail I’s queen, financed the Golden Iwan of the <b>Fatima</b> Masumeh Shrine at Qom in 1519, built her husband’s tomb at Ardabil and supported Tahmasp’s accession in 1524.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li><li><blockquote>As Imam al-Hadi ila al-Haqq he settled their disputes and founded a Zaydi state, establishing the doctrine that any learned descendant of Ali and <b>Fatima</b> who claimed the imamate by rising against injustice could be imam.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaydi-imamate-of-yemen">Zaydi Imamate of Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
